### Rules for Frontend

- You must pay attention to visual clarity and contrast. Do not place white text on a white background.
- You must ensure the UX is pleasant. Boxes should grow to fit their contents, etc. 
- When asking the user for sensitive information - you must use password style text-entry boxes in the UI.
- You should use Next.js as it works best with Vercel. 
- This frontend will ultimately be deployed on Vercel, but it should be possible to test locally. 
- Always provide users with a way to run the created UI once you have created it.
- The color theme should be beige, dark grey, and black.
- Use a warm beige background, dark grey body text, and black primary buttons.
- Give the interface a calm, welcoming appearance with generous spacing.
- Use rounded message bubbles and clearly distinguish user and assistant messages.
- Make the chat interface work comfortably on both phones and desktop screens.
- Show a loading indicator while waiting for a reply and a clear message if a request fails.

### Documentation references
- Consult the official Next.js documentation when implementing frontend features:
  https://nextjs.org/docs
- Consult the official Vercel documentation when configuring deployment:
  https://vercel.com/docs
- Read the relevant documentation pages before making implementation decisions.
- Use guidance that matches the versions installed in this project.